def fisher_kpp_speed : Informal where
  id := "fisher-kpp-speed"
  label := "Fisher-KPP wave of advance of an advantageous gene"
  statement := "An advantageous allele spreading through a continuous population advances as a travelling wave whose speed is set by the selection coefficient and the diffusion (dispersal) rate.  [u_t = D u_{xx} + s u(1-u); travelling waves u(x-ct) exist iff c \\ge c^* = 2\\sqrt{sD}; compactly supported initial data spread at asymptotic speed c^*]"
  source := "Ronald A. Fisher (1937), The wave of advance of advantageous genes"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["one", "contingent"]

def fisher_sex_ratio : Informal where
  id := "fisher-sex-ratio"
  label := "Fisher's principle of equal investment in the sexes"
  statement := "Under random mating and autosomal inheritance, selection drives parents to invest equally in sons and daughters, which gives a 1:1 sex ratio when the two cost the same.  [ESS allocation: N_m c_m = N_f c_f; with c_m = c_f the unbeatable primary sex ratio is 1:1]"
  source := "Ronald A. Fisher (1930), The Genetical Theory of Natural Selection"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["one", "contingent"]

def fisher_infinitesimal : Informal where
  id := "fisher-infinitesimal"
  label := "Fisher's infinitesimal model of quantitative inheritance"
  statement := "Continuous traits determined by many Mendelian loci of small additive effect produce the observed correlations between relatives, so biometric and Mendelian inheritance are consistent.  [Cov(z_i, z_j) = 2\\Theta_{ij} V_A + \\Delta_{ij} V_D; in the limit of M \\to \\infty loci the offspring trait given parents is Gaussian with mean (z_1+z_2)/2 and segregation variance V_0(1-\\bar{F}), with error O(1/\\sqrt{M}) (Barton, Etheridge and Veber 2017)]"
  source := "Ronald A. Fisher (1919), The Correlation between Relatives on the Supposition of Mendelian Inheritance"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["one", "contingent"]

def turing_ddi : Informal where
  id := "turing-ddi"
  label := "Turing diffusion-driven instability"
  statement := "A homogeneous steady state of two reacting chemicals that is stable without diffusion can become unstable to spatial perturbations when the chemicals diffuse at different rates, spontaneously producing a pattern.  [u_t = f(u,v) + D_u \\nabla^2 u, v_t = g(u,v) + D_v \\nabla^2 v; with tr J < 0 and det J > 0, instability at some wavenumber k iff D_v f_u + D_u g_v > 2\\sqrt{D_u D_v \\det J}, which requires D_u \\ne D_v]"
  source := "Alan Turing (1952), The chemical basis of morphogenesis"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["one", "contingent"]

def turing_wavelength : Informal where
  id := "turing-wavelength"
  label := "Turing wavelength selection"
  statement := "The spacing of a Turing pattern at onset is set by the reaction rates and diffusivities, not by the size of the tissue, so changing these parameters predictably changes the number of repeated elements.  [at onset k_c^2 = \\sqrt{\\det J/(D_u D_v)}, \\lambda_c = 2\\pi/k_c; under uniform rescaling of D, \\lambda \\propto \\sqrt{D}]"
  source := "Alan Turing (1952), The chemical basis of morphogenesis"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["one", "contingent"]

def turing_1941_factor_principle : Informal where
  id := "turing-1941-factor-principle"
  label := "The factor principle: posterior odds are prior odds times the likelihood ratio"
  statement := "The odds in favour of a theory after seeing some data equal the odds before seeing it, multiplied by the ratio of the probability of the data if the theory is true to the probability of the data if the theory is false.  [O(H \\mid D) = O(H) \\cdot \\frac{P(D \\mid H)}{P(D \\mid \\neg H)}; Turing calls the ratio 'the factor for the theory on account of the data']"
  source := "Alan Turing (1941), The Applications of Probability to Cryptography"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["identity"]

def turing_1941_deciban_additivity : Informal where
  id := "turing-1941-deciban-additivity"
  label := "Decibans: independent pieces of evidence add on a log-odds scale"
  statement := "If several pieces of data are independent of one another both when the theory is true and when it is false, the evidence they give together, measured in decibans (ten times the base-10 logarithm of the factor), is the sum of the decibans each gives alone; one deciban is the evidence that multiplies the odds by the tenth root of ten.  [\\mathrm{db}(D_1,\\dots,D_n) = \\sum_i 10\\log_{10}\\frac{P(D_i \\mid H)}{P(D_i \\mid \\neg H)}, given P(D_1,\\dots,D_n \\mid H) = \\prod_i P(D_i \\mid H) and likewise under \\neg H]"
  source := "Alan Turing (1941), The Applications of Probability to Cryptography"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["identity", "contingent"]

def turing_1941_expected_factor_false_hypothesis : Informal where
  id := "turing-1941-expected-factor-false-hypothesis"
  label := "The expected Bayes factor in favour of a false hypothesis is exactly 1"
  statement := "If a hypothesis is false, the average over possible observations of the factor in its favour (the probability of the observation if the hypothesis were true divided by its probability given the truth) is exactly one; so evidence can only favour a false hypothesis on average by sometimes favouring it strongly and usually counting against it.  [\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\frac{P(E \\mid H)}{P(E \\mid \\neg H)} \\,\\middle|\\, \\neg H\\right] = \\sum_E P(E \\mid \\neg H)\\frac{P(E \\mid H)}{P(E \\mid \\neg H)} = \\sum_E P(E \\mid H) = 1]"
  source := "Alan Turing (1985), Weight of evidence: a brief survey (Bayesian Statistics 2, pp. 249-270)"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["identity"]

def turing_1941_expected_weight_nonnegative : Informal where
  id := "turing-1941-expected-weight-nonnegative"
  label := "The expected weight of evidence in favour of the truth is never negative"
  statement := "If a hypothesis is true, the average weight of evidence (log Bayes factor) that an observation gives in its favour is non-negative, and is zero only when no possible observation discriminates the hypothesis from its rival; so, on average, gathering evidence moves one toward the truth.  [\\mathbb{E}[W(H{:}E) \\mid H] = \\sum_E P(E \\mid H)\\log\\frac{P(E \\mid H)}{P(E \\mid \\neg H)} \\ge 0, with equality iff P(E \\mid H) = P(E \\mid \\neg H) for all E; this is the Kullback-Leibler divergence D(P_H \\| P_{\\neg H})]"
  source := "Alan Turing (1985), Weight of evidence: a brief survey (Bayesian Statistics 2, pp. 249-270)"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["identity"]

def turing_1941_weight_variance_twice_mean : Informal where
  id := "turing-1941-weight-variance-twice-mean"
  label := "If the weight of evidence is normally distributed, its variance is twice its mean"
  statement := "If, when a hypothesis is true, the weight of evidence an observation gives in its favour is normally distributed, then, measured in natural units, its variance equals twice its mean, and when the hypothesis is false the weight has the mirror-image distribution with mean minus that value; so the spread of evidence is much larger than its expectation suggests.  [W(H{:}E) \\mid H \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2) in natural bans \\Rightarrow \\sigma^2 = 2\\mu and W(H{:}E) \\mid \\neg H \\sim N(-\\mu, \\sigma^2); in decibans \\sigma \\approx 3\\sqrt{\\mu}]"
  source := "Alan Turing (1985), Weight of evidence: a brief survey (Bayesian Statistics 2, pp. 249-270)"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["one", "contingent"]

def kimura_fixation_time : Informal where
  id := "kimura-fixation-time"
  label := "Mean time to fixation of a neutral mutant"
  statement := "A neutral mutant that does go to fixation takes on average about four times the effective population size, in generations, to do so.  [\\bar{t}_1(p) = -\\frac{4N_e(1-p)\\ln(1-p)}{p}; as p = 1/(2N) \\to 0, \\bar{t}_1 \\to 4N_e generations]"
  source := "Motoo Kimura (1969), The average number of generations until fixation of a mutant gene in a finite population"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["one", "contingent"]

def kimura_heterozygosity : Informal where
  id := "kimura-heterozygosity"
  label := "Neutral equilibrium heterozygosity (Kimura-Crow)"
  statement := "Under neutral mutation to new alleles and drift, expected heterozygosity reaches an equilibrium that rises with the product of effective population size and mutation rate.  [H = \\frac{\\theta}{1+\\theta}, \\theta = 4N_e\\mu (infinite-alleles model)]"
  source := "Motoo Kimura (1964), The number of alleles that can be maintained in a finite population"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["one", "contingent"]

def hamilton_rule : Informal where
  id := "hamilton-rule"
  label := "Hamilton's rule"
  statement := "A gene for social behaviour increases in frequency when the benefit to the recipient, weighted by genetic relatedness, exceeds the cost to the actor.  [\\Delta \\bar{p} > 0 \\iff r b - c > 0, with r, b, c defined as regression coefficients (Queller form via the Price equation)]"
  source := "W. D. Hamilton (1964), The genetical evolution of social behaviour. I"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["several", "identity", "contingent"]

def hamilton_lmc : Informal where
  id := "hamilton-lmc"
  label := "Local mate competition sex ratio"
  statement := "When a few mothers lay eggs on a patch and their offspring mate there before the daughters disperse, the evolutionarily stable sex ratio is female-biased, and the bias weakens as the number of mothers grows.  [unbeatable proportion of males x^* = \\frac{n-1}{2n} for n foundresses per patch (diploid case)]"
  source := "W. D. Hamilton (1967), Extraordinary Sex Ratios"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["one", "contingent"]

def maynardsmith_ess : Informal where
  id := "maynardsmith-ess"
  label := "Evolutionarily stable strategy conditions"
  statement := "A strategy is evolutionarily stable if, once common, it cannot be invaded by any rare alternative strategy.  [I is an ESS iff for all J \\ne I: E(I,I) > E(J,I), or E(I,I) = E(J,I) and E(I,J) > E(J,J); every ESS is an asymptotically stable rest point of the replicator dynamics (Taylor and Jonker 1978), not conversely]"
  source := "John Maynard Smith (1973), The Logic of Animal Conflict"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["one", "contingent"]

def maynardsmith_hawk_dove : Informal where
  id := "maynardsmith-hawk-dove"
  label := "Hawk-Dove mixed ESS"
  statement := "When the cost of injury exceeds the value of the contested resource, the stable outcome is a mixture in which animals escalate with probability equal to the ratio of resource value to injury cost.  [payoffs E(H,H) = (V-C)/2, E(H,D) = V, E(D,H) = 0, E(D,D) = V/2; for C > V the unique ESS plays Hawk with probability p^* = V/C]"
  source := "John Maynard Smith (1982), Evolution and the Theory of Games"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["one", "contingent"]

def maynardsmith_war_of_attrition : Informal where
  id := "maynardsmith-war-of-attrition"
  label := "War of attrition ESS"
  statement := "In contests settled by display, where the animal willing to persist longer wins and costs grow with time, the stable strategy is to choose a persistence time at random from an exponential distribution whose mean equals the resource value.  [with cost linear in time at unit rate and resource value V, the ESS persistence time has density p(x) = \\frac{1}{V} e^{-x/V}]"
  source := "John Maynard Smith (1974), The theory of games and the evolution of animal conflicts"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["one", "contingent"]

def hardy_weinberg : Informal where
  id := "hardy-weinberg"
  label := "Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium"
  statement := "In an infinitely large, randomly mating diploid population with no selection, mutation or migration, the genotype frequencies at a biallelic autosomal locus reach p^2, 2pq, q^2 after a single generation and stay there, with allele frequencies unchanged.  [f(AA) = p^2,\\; f(Aa) = 2pq,\\; f(aa) = q^2,\\quad p + q = 1]"
  source := "G. H. Hardy and Wilhelm Weinberg (1908), Mendelian Proportions in a Mixed Population"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["one", "contingent"]

def price_equation : Informal where
  id := "price-equation"
  label := "The Price equation (covariance form)"
  statement := "For any population partitioned into entities i with fitness w_i and character value z_i, the change in the population mean character between generations equals the covariance of fitness with character plus the fitness-weighted mean of within-entity transmission change, each divided by mean fitness.  [\\bar{w}\\,\\Delta\\bar{z} = \\mathrm{Cov}(w_i, z_i) + \\mathrm{E}(w_i\\,\\Delta z_i)]"
  source := "George R. Price (1970), Selection and Covariance"
  status := "literature-proved"
  tags := ["identity", "contingent"]

